A||||7||CUTTER||humming from front tires......what is it?||||1||
Z||000000||CUTTER||05-13-2000||11:45 PM||||I noticed just a few days ago that there was a humming comming from my front end. I heard the same noise 2 months ago and it ended up to be bearings, but I just replaced them like 1 month ago. My front tires are pretty much bald on the one side because my alignment is off. Could it be that? the humming sound gets a little louder and faster, the faster I go. What could it be?<BR>Thanks, Shane blue87GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||Zman302||05-14-2000||12:22 AM||||the noise could still be the bearings even though they were just replaced.  Inproper torque on the axel nut is a big problem.  also check the brake pads, i had the pads that had the shimms on the back for quietness and must of got them hot and the adhesive came loose and the pad sat in the caliper crooked and made a whirring noise that got louder with speed.  Hope this helps and good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ed||05-14-2000||12:34 AM||||You can check if it is tire noise by putting some other tires and wheels on.  If you know someone who also has a Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroshop||05-14-2000||10:22 AM||||Experience tells me that you need a wheel alignment and new tires. Get your tires first and do not drive it until you have a four wheel alignment.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||05-14-2000||11:19 AM||||I agree with Fieroshop, however, I would reinspect the wheel bearing for sloppiness and retorque them if needed. Easy, cheap insurance before spending the big bucks.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||05-14-2000||11:33 AM||||I'd go that just one further.<P>pull the bearings and inspect them. If they were not installed right they may be ruined. If they are good then put them back an properly torque them. <P>Keep in mind there are 2(TWO) cotter holes in the spindle. You only back off the nut untill the first hole that lines up. Do Not worry if it's not the same hole it was in to start with.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||05-14-2000||02:46 PM||||Yep, Orgre's right, that's what I meant when I said inspect them. Can't see them until you pull the rotor off. Repack them with that special high temperature wheel bearing grease made for cars with disc brakes too.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Big O||05-14-2000||04:20 PM||||Checking the wheel bearings is certainly worth the effort to be sure they are properly installed, but concentrate on the tires.  Tire noise can be caused by a number of issues, all of which will usually entail replacing the tires.  If you have excessive wear on the sides there is no question you have an alignment problem and it needs to be fixed SOON as the tire wear will get worse.  Rotating the tires front to back may help a little, but you may have a wear pattern set up that will contimue to make noise.  Tires also develop what is called kinisity problems which can cause the car to drift to the left or the right.  This is a manufacturers defect that should be covered by warranty if you bought tires from a reputable tire dealer. To see if this is the problem, try moving the tires around the car.  Switching from one side to the other will usually change the direction of the drift or pull.  When you get this, replace the tires.  Cupped or flat spotted tires will also cause ride disturbances and can only be cured by replacing the tires.  The best thing to do to prevent these conditions is to buy quality tires and to have them rotated and balanced at least twice a year.||||reg||1
